europe strategy for improving lives the  economy and the environment in the  Baltic Sea region is up for discussion  at the European General Affairs Council  on November 19 the strategies at  cornerstone of the Lithuanian presidency  has been a model of EU regional  interaction since 2009 it aims to boost  cooperation between the eight EU  countries around the sea and the more  than 85 million people who live there  the strategy tackles environmental  problems economic disparities between  richer nations like Sweden and Germany  and neighbors like Latvia or Poland and  social challenges part of it addresses  ways to save the sea from pollution for  example some 50,000 tons of chemical  munitions have been dumped in the Baltic  since World War two improving transport  links for greater trade and prosperity  is another focus the region's current  road and rail networks are incomplete  and need upgrading one project the rail  baltika system will link helsinki  through the Baltic States and via Warsaw  to Berlin providing for rail travel and  freight deliveries to the rest of Europe  the strategy also aims to boost  economies through new technologies like  this project in Vilnius where students  are building innovative means of  transport for moving around in a  sustainable way at the talks in brussels  the ministers will take stock of the  strategy and how the challenges of  limited resources and financing are  being addressed
 

